  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nHighly-amplified ridging spanning from central Canada down into the \nMidwest and Great Lakes will continue to slide east through the \nremainder of this weekend into early next week. Multiple waves/jet \nmaxima embedded in the mid/upper-level flow look to progress \noverhead through Sunday. Meanwhile, south-southwest low-level flow \nwill advect an increasingly moist airmass into the Great Lakes as a \nwarm front looks to lift into the Great Lakes and drape across the \nmiddle of the state by early next week. More organized shortwaves \nmay slide over the region during the middle of next week before \namplified ridging appears it will grasp much of the central CONUS \nheading into the holiday weekend.\n\nForecast Discussion:\n\nAs the previous forecaster discussed, many rounds of showers and \nperhaps a few storms are expected across northern Michigan over the \nnext several days with the primary concern being localized heavy \nrainfall potential late tonight into Sunday. Widespread light rain \nacross Wisconsin and Lake Michigan continues to work towards \nnorthern Michigan this afternoon with latest obs/guidance \nreinforcing belief that measurable rainfall greater than a few \nhundredths of an inch is unlikely from this activity before sunset. \nInitially dry low-level air may make it difficult for falling precip \nto reach the surface at first, and ongoing rain may continue to \nweaken over the next few hours as it works into western portions of \nthe area.\n\nRegardless, rain chances/coverage is expected to increase with time \ntonight -- especially late tonight through Sunday morning. Warm \nadvection will help generate repeated, widespread showers and a few \nstorms along/north of the aforementioned boundary. The main question \nat this time will be exactly where these showers will form and train \nas latest guidance creeps the favored corridor slightly further \nsouth with time. That said, the best chances for steady rain will \ncome along and south of M-72. PWATs around 1.8\" will support \nefficient rainfall, and locations that see training showers could \nsee 1\"+ of rainfall by Sunday afternoon. While potential is low, \nlocalized flooding cannot be ruled out.\n\nLooking ahead, additional rounds of showers and a few storms will be \npossible through much of the upcoming week as aforementioned waves \nwork across the region. Seasonably warm temperatures are also \nexpected across the area through the first half of the week. While \nrain chances may impact the extent of high temperatures, afternoon \nreadings may warm into the mid to upper 80s by Tuesday.",
